  "prompt_text": "The figure explains how vertical wind shear and soil moisture together influence whether thunderstorms develop. It contrasts three situations using cartoon clouds and rain. In the first case, a tall cumulonimbus forms under strong wind shear, with arrows showing that wind speed and direction change with height. This “high shear” setting makes storm initiation more likely, and when the shear direction is negative, initiation is shown as favored over dry soil. The second case shows another storm under positive wind-shear direction, but here the preferred surface condition is wet soil. Both upper panels therefore suggest that strong shear supports thunderstorm growth, while the sign of the shear determines whether dry or wet land is the more favorable trigger.\n\nThe bottom panel shows weak shear, represented by small, shallow cumulus clouds instead of a deep thunderstorm. With low wind-shear magnitude, storms appear much less likely to initiate, even if the land surface would otherwise be favorable. Overall, the image highlights that both the magnitude and direction of wind shear shape where convection starts, and that soil moisture can shift the preferred location for thunderstorm initiation.",
